WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.913
Watch this ten-component
assembly, without cuts.

2
00:00:03.913 --> 00:00:06.900
The robot must assemble
every active component,

3
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.000
but the task does not prescribe an order.
Notice the long reach across the board,

4
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.750
and the small contacts
used to turn the nut.

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.988
There is no separate planner
choosing a sequence of skills.

6
00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:24.913
These actions come from
one learned policy.

7
00:00:24.913 --> 00:00:28.975
The task reward arrives only when
all ten assemblies are complete,

8
00:00:28.975 --> 00:00:31.225
not after each intermediate step.

9
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.038
If success is so far away, where
does useful experience come from?

10
00:00:37.038 --> 00:00:42.013
Alpha NIST combines diverse starting
states, shared training across assemblies,

11
00:00:42.013 --> 00:00:44.100
and large-scale simulation.

12
00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:49.115
The NIST board brings
together twenty assemblies,

13
00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.903
with parts as small as four millimeters.

14
00:00:51.903 --> 00:00:57.315
A grasp that lifts a part may still block
its insertion. With several parts present,

15
00:00:57.315 --> 00:01:01.465
the robot must also work around
clutter and neighboring assemblies.

16
00:01:04.215 --> 00:01:08.690
We use a binary task reward for completed
assembly. Reaching, grasping,

17
00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:12.340
and partial insertion receive
no assembly-progress reward.

18
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.165
Separate control costs discourage
large actions, rapid motion,

19
00:01:16.165 --> 00:01:19.690
and excessive torque.
The objective specifies the result,

20
00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.015
not a rewarded sequence of maneuvers.

21
00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:27.203
A binary objective does not mean
always starting from loose parts.

22
00:01:27.203 --> 00:01:32.565
A cached bank provides valid states, from
objects on the table to in-hand grasps and

23
00:01:32.565 --> 00:01:36.065
partial assemblies. These
are starting conditions,

24
00:01:36.065 --> 00:01:39.065
not demonstrations or prescribed stages.

25
00:01:39.065 --> 00:01:41.915
The policy must still
discover how to finish.

26
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:49.227
Which states should the learner
revisit? Success-guided sampling favors

27
00:01:49.227 --> 00:01:53.252
intermediate recent success:
neither almost always solved,

28
00:01:53.252 --> 00:01:57.828
nor almost always failed. Every cached
state retains some chance of being

29
00:01:57.828 --> 00:02:02.452
sampled. This choice spans
the entire assembly-and-reset bank,

30
00:02:02.452 --> 00:02:06.065
selecting both the geometry
and the starting condition.

31
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.915
For single-component experiments, one
policy trains across all twenty tasks

32
00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:18.490
using PPO. It observes robot
state and surface point clouds,

33
00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:23.315
without a discrete task label. Different
geometries share the same learner and

34
00:02:23.315 --> 00:02:24.965
completion objective.

35
00:02:26.265 --> 00:02:29.390
Does sharing help?
With binary task rewards,

36
00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.553
the shared policy reaches ninety-eight
point two percent training success,

37
00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:37.928
versus eighty-one point four averaged
over twenty separate policies.

38
00:02:37.928 --> 00:02:43.127
These results include partially assembled
starts, with budgets matched per run.

39
00:02:43.127 --> 00:02:47.415
The tested dense reward does not
show the same benefit from sharing.

40
00:02:50.215 --> 00:02:54.940
At the largest scale, binary task rewards
reach ninety-seven point four percent with

41
00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.565
success-guided sampling, versus
eighty-four point three with uniform.

42
00:02:59.565 --> 00:03:05.065
Now add dense shaping: seventy-seven point
four and thirty-four point three percent.

43
00:03:05.065 --> 00:03:10.215
Here, sparsity matters too: binary task
rewards outperform the tested shaping

44
00:03:10.215 --> 00:03:11.890
under both samplers.

45
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:18.790
At short horizons, we observe unexpected
dexterity and varied uses of the gripper,

46
00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.640
beyond simply picking
up and placing parts.

47
00:03:23.265 --> 00:03:27.627
This peg is held near its center,
leaving little room for insertion.

48
00:03:27.627 --> 00:03:32.515
The gripper sets it down, then regrasps
near one end to expose enough shaft.

49
00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.990
The gripper uses the board to change
the connector's orientation,

50
00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:45.265
then lifts it into the socket. Surface
contact extends what the fingers can do in

51
00:03:45.265 --> 00:03:46.440
free space.

52
00:03:48.265 --> 00:03:52.178
On the small nut, the fingertip
repeatedly contacts one side,

53
00:03:52.178 --> 00:03:53.940
turning it along the thread.

54
00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:59.440
In another example, the gripper changes
its position around the nut to keep

55
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.465
turning it.

56
00:04:02.465 --> 00:04:07.490
Here, the outside of a finger contacts
the nut, advancing it through short turns.

57
00:04:10.265 --> 00:04:14.540
Here, the gripper circles the nut,
turning it through successive contacts.

58
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.640
At longer horizons, the more surprising
behavior is how these local maneuvers fit

59
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.090
together. The robot
tosses loose parts aside,

60
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:28.653
opening space to work on other
components first. It then links grasping,

61
00:04:28.653 --> 00:04:34.390
reorientation, and insertion across the
task, without a prescribed assembly order.

62
00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:38.565
These selected runs show how local
dexterity can combine into a longer

63
00:04:38.565 --> 00:04:39.565
sequence.

64
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:49.225
All results are in simulation. Physical
transfer and continuous twenty-component

65
00:04:49.225 --> 00:04:52.975
assembly under one terminal
objective remain open.

66
00:04:52.975 --> 00:04:57.325
What we see here is that diverse starts
and shared experience can support several

67
00:04:57.325 --> 00:05:01.200
kinds of dexterous behavior under
a common completion objective.
